.TH GETADDRINFO 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
getaddrinfo, freeaddrinfo, gai_strerror, getnameinfo \- resolve IPv4 names and addresses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <helios/unix/netdb.h>

int getaddrinfo(const char *node, const char *service,
                const struct addrinfo *hints, struct addrinfo **result);
void freeaddrinfo(struct addrinfo *result);
const char *gai_strerror(int error);
int getnameinfo(const struct sockaddr *address, socklen_t length,
                char *host, socklen_t hostsize,
                char *service, socklen_t servicesize, int flags);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
getaddrinfo returns an allocated linked list of addresses. Zero-initialize
hints, then select AF_INET or AF_UNSPEC and SOCK_STREAM or SOCK_DGRAM as needed.
Only IPv4, TCP and UDP are supported. Each result supplies ai_family,
ai_socktype, ai_protocol, ai_addr and ai_addrlen for socket/connect/bind.
Try successive addresses when a connection fails; close each failed socket.
Release the whole list with freeaddrinfo, which also accepts NULL.
.PP
The service can be a decimal port or a service database name. With NULL node,
AI_PASSIVE requests the wildcard address for bind; otherwise the loopback
address is used. Both node and service cannot be NULL.
.PP
Supported hint flags are AI_PASSIVE, AI_CANONNAME (request the canonical name
in the first result), AI_NUMERICHOST and AI_NUMERICSERV (disable the corresponding
name lookup). At most 32 distinct IPv4 addresses are collected; unspecified
socket type can produce both TCP and UDP results.
.PP
getnameinfo writes NUL-terminated host/service strings into caller-owned buffers.
Either output may be NULL, but not both. NI_NUMERICHOST and NI_NUMERICSERV
request numeric results. Without NI_NAMEREQD, unavailable reverse DNS falls
back to a numeric address. NI_DGRAM selects UDP service names instead of TCP.
.PP
These calls are synchronous and can wait for DNS. Use hu_resolve for lookup
outside a responsive application's main task. Local hosts-file entries take
precedence over DNS. DNS supports search names, CNAMEs and TCP fallback.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, otherwise an EAI error code. Describe it with gai_strerror;
its returned string is borrowed and must not be freed. Errors include
EAI_NONAME, EAI_AGAIN, EAI_FAMILY, EAI_SOCKTYPE, EAI_SERVICE, EAI_BADFLAGS,
EAI_MEMORY and EAI_OVERFLOW. Do not use perror for an EAI error.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <helios/unix/netdb.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int list_addresses(const char *name)
{
    struct addrinfo hints = {0}, *list, *p;
    char host[64];
    int error;
    hints.ai_family = AF_INET;
    hints.ai_socktype = SOCK_STREAM;
    error = getaddrinfo(name, "443", &hints, &list);
    if (error) {
        fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", gai_strerror(error));
        return error;
    }
    for (p = list; p; p = p->ai_next) {
        error = getnameinfo(p->ai_addr, p->ai_addrlen,
                            host, sizeof(host), 0, 0, NI_NUMERICHOST);
        if (!error) puts(host);
    }
    freeaddrinfo(list);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.SH FILES
/helios/etc/hosts, /helios/etc/resolv.cnf
.SH SEE ALSO
hu_resolve(3), hu_tls(3), helios-unix(7)
